# A sample /STARTUP.BAT -- copy it to / and edit. It runs at power-on.
# If one you wrote wedges the machine, turn it off in F7 > Run STARTUP.BAT,
# or start the emulator with --no-startup.bat. Your own copy is never
# overwritten.
# Aliases live in RAM, so they are defined here every boot.

# the CP/M programs, each through a .SUB on A: that ends in EXIT,
# so quitting the program comes all the way back to this shell
ALIAS MBASIC CPM K-MBAS
ALIAS TURBO  CPM K-TURBO
ALIAS WS     CPM K-WS

# odds and ends
ALIAS LL DIR A

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSOLE COLOURS.  COLOR (or COLOUR) sets the foreground and, if you give
# a second value, the background.  Both are palette indices IN HEX, and it
# clears the screen as it takes effect -- which is why it belongs near the
# top of a startup file rather than the bottom.
#
#     COLOR fg
#     COLOR fg bg
#
# The palette is the C64's sixteen, in the C64's own order:
#
#     0  black          8  orange
#     1  white          9  brown
#     2  red            A  light red
#     3  cyan           B  dark grey
#     4  purple         C  grey
#     5  green          D  light green
#     6  blue           E  light blue
#     7  yellow         F  light grey
#
# The machine's own is yellow on blue, which is COLOR 7 6.  Some others
# worth trying:
#
#     COLOR D 0      light green on black   (a VDU)
#     COLOR 1 6      white on blue          (plainer than the default)
#     COLOR 8 0      amber on black         (a terminal)
#     COLOR 0 F      black on light grey    (paper)
#
# Uncomment one, or add your own:
#
# COLOR D 0
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KEYBOARD LAYOUT IS NOT SET HERE, and cannot be.  This file is the
# guest's shell script; the USB keyboard is translated by the host below
# it -- on the Pi by Circle, before the ROM has run at all -- so nothing
# written in here can reach it.
#
# On the Pi it is cmdline.txt in the root of the SD card, which must
# contain exactly this and NO trailing newline (Circle splits its command
# line on spaces only, so a newline stays inside the value and the whole
# option is silently ignored):
#
#     keymap=us
#
# The layouts Circle carries, and the only names it accepts:
#
#     us    United States (the default this machine ships)
#     uk    United Kingdom
#     de    Germany          <- what it falls back to when the option
#                               is missing or malformed
#     fr    France
#     es    Spain
#     it    Italy
#     dv    Dvorak
#
# On the desktop the layout is the host operating system's own; the
# emulator does not translate keys itself.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
